// The `std` package root.
//
// `std` is the floor a program stands on when it is running on an operating
// system: files, processes, text and containers. It is an **ordinary package**
// — a directory of `.nest` files with this one as its root — and it ships with
// the compiler, which is the only thing that makes it different from any other
// dependency: one `std` per `nestc`, so a program never has to work out which
// one it is being built against.
//
// **It is not linked automatically.** Nothing here is in scope until a program
// writes `import <std/...>`, and a program that writes none gets none of it.
// `core` is the package that is always there; this one is asked for. That is
// also why `std` may use `core` freely and `core` may never use `std`: `core`
// has to work on a target with no operating system at all.
//
// This file only re-exports, and it re-exports **namespaces**: `import <std/io>`
// names the `io` namespace below. The layout is `core`'s, for the same reason —
// a root that is a table of contents rather than a pile of members.

@public io          :: import "io/io.nest"
@public fs          :: import "fs/fs.nest"
@public os          :: import "os/os.nest"
@public text        :: import "text/text.nest"
@public collections :: import "collections/collections.nest"
@public net         :: import "net/net.nest"
@public mem         :: import "mem.nest"
@public serialize   :: import "serialize/serialize.nest"

// `os/sys.nest` gets **no re-export**, and that is what makes it internal (see
// `os/os.nest`).
